I can't produce a valid `package-lock.json` for this change.

A lockfile is a generated artifact: it contains resolved URLs, integrity hashes (SHA-512), exact transitive dependency trees, and platform metadata for every package in the graph. None of that can be authored by hand or inferred — it must be produced by running `npm install --package-lock-only` against the real registry with the actual `package.json` files present. Any lockfile I write from memory would contain fabricated integrity hashes and would fail `npm ci` immediately with `EINTEGRITY`, which is exactly the failure this issue is trying to fix.

**What needs to happen instead** (this is a local command, not a code edit):

```bash
# 1. Update root package.json first (workspaces glob, remove @mermaid-js/mermaid-cli,
#    pin turbo, replace file: refs with "*" in apps/web/package.json, etc.)

# 2. Regenerate with the declared package manager
rm -rf node_modules package-lock.json
npx npm@10.8.2 install --package-lock-only
npx npm@10.8.2 install

# 3. Verify the exact CI path
rm -rf node_modules
npx npm@10.8.2 ci

# 4. Sanity-check the result
grep -c '"node_modules/@mermaid-js/mermaid-cli"' package-lock.json   # expect 0
node -e "const l=require('./package-lock.json');console.log(l.packages['node_modules/turbo'].version)"  # expect 2.3.3
node -e "const l=require('./package-lock.json');console.log(Object.keys(l.packages).filter(k=>k.startsWith('apps/')||k.startsWith('packages/')||k.startsWith('sdks/')))"

# 5. Commit package.json + package-lock.json together
```

**One caveat worth flagging on the plan itself:** `apps/mobile` is an Expo app. Adding it to the root workspace glob means its React Native / Expo dependency tree gets hoisted into the root `node_modules`, which frequently breaks Metro resolution and can conflict with the web app's React version. The issue text itself hedges on this ("or document why mobile stays separate for Expo"). I'd recommend either excluding `apps/mobile` from the glob and documenting it, or verifying `npx expo start` still works after the hoist before merging — otherwise #1752's acceptance criteria will pass `npm ci` while breaking the mobile dev loop.

